reject malformed node/edge entries early and fail cleanly on schema errors before dereferencing `nodes`/`edges` [Task 1][Task 4]\n1374-- symptom: heading/anchor discovery commands fail during graph seeding -> cause: the regex is more complicated than the task requires -> fix: use simpler `rg` patterns when you only need section headings or labels for locator audits [Task 3]\n1375:- symptom: graph closeouts overstate mathematical certainty -> cause: the graph is treated as a proof artifact instead of a provenance/navigation layer -> fix: state explicitly that the graph records the current support status and does not independently re-prove the mathematics [Task 2][Task 3]\n1376-\n1377:# Task Group: source-workspace approval-gated local manuscript review\n1378-\n1379-scope: read-only local manuscript inspection in `writing/prl_stabilizer_region_algebras`, with explicit source-of-truth confirmation, draft-marker audits, and no edits before approval\n1380:applies_to: cwd=source-workspace; reuse_rule=reuse for local manuscript-review and scope-calibration tasks in this checkout, but treat exact draft markers and line-level issues as checkout-specific\n1381-\n1382:## Task 1: inspect the local manuscript read-only and calibrate review scope before any edits\n1383-\n1384-### rollout_summary_files\n--\n1392-## User preferences\n1393-\n1394:- when the user said \"don‘t do anything without approval\" -> keep manuscript review read-only until they explicitly approve edits to durable artifacts [Task 1]\n1395-- when the user corrected the source to \"nono there is the local git repo\" -> confirm the local repo/source-of-truth path before falling back to browser or Overleaf workflows [Task 1]\n1396-\n1397-## Reusable knowledge\n1398-\n1399:- `writing/prl_stabilizer_region_algebras/main.tex` was the live source of truth for this review; older compiled PDF/log outputs were stale enough that they should be treated only as secondary evidence [Task 1]\n1400-- The draft currently contains many visible `\\\\LS{...}` markers, explicit placeholder text such as `SUGGESTED PROOF`, and an undefined-reference mismatch where `thm:define_state` is defined but `thm:define-state` is referenced later [Task 1]\n1401:- A useful review framing here is to separate rigorous claims from conjectural or heuristic material and to audit for dangling theorem references, overbroad theorem statements, and stale compiled outputs before discussing stylistic cleanup [Task 1]\n1402-\n1403-## Failures and how to do differently\n1404-\n1405:- symptom: the review starts from the wrong source channel -> cause: browser/Overleaf assumptions outran the actual local checkout -> fix: identify the local repo path first and stay on the checked-out `.tex` source unless the user explicitly asks for a browser workflow [Task 1]\n1406:- symptom: review conclusions lean on stale PDF/log output -> cause: the compiled artifacts are older than the edited `.tex` draft -> fix: prioritize the source `.tex` and use current logs only as secondary evidence [Task 1]\n1407-\n1408-# Task Group: <original-home>/Projects/current/vsn finite-group ribbon eigenstates from the string-net vacuum\n\n-- skill --\n---\nname: aristotle-cli\ndescription: Use when Codex should interact with the Aristotle CLI to submit Lean projects or papers, request sorry filling or formalization runs, inspect job status, and download results for iterative project work.\n---\n\n# Aristotle CLI\n\nUse Aristotle for remote Lean/project runs when the local repo is ready but needs automated proof search, sorry filling, or formalization help.\n\n## Workflow\n\n1. Confirm the target project or paper from the current workspace.\n2. Check the local build or project state first. Prefer a narrow local `lake build` or equivalent before submitting anything remote.\n3. Use the bundled wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh` unless `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` is already known to be exported in the current process.\n4. Submit the smallest useful task prompt. Keep it concrete and tied to the files at hand.\n5. Wait for the result when that is useful. Otherwise poll or list jobs.\n6. Download artifacts, inspect the diffs, and rerun local verification.\n\n## Commands\n\nUse the commands in [references/aristotle-cli.md](references/aristotle-cli.md).\n\n## Operating rules\n\n- Prefer `aristotle submit` for Lean project work, especially sorry filling or proof search.\n- Prefer `aristotle formalize` when the input is a paper or TeX source that should become a Lean artifact.\n- Use `aristotle list` to inspect recent jobs and `aristotle download` to retrieve results.\n- Keep prompts specific: name the file, theorem, section, or proof obligation instead of asking for broad improvement.\n- If a run fails, inspect the local artifact and re-submit only after the prompt or project state is corrected.\n- Do not print or log the API key.\n- If authentication fails but `~/.bashrc` contains the key, do not assume sourcing `.bashrc` works. Many `.bashrc` files return early for noninteractive shells. Use `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\n## When to escalate back to the user\n\n- The CLI is missing, or authentication still fails after trying `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n- The target repo is not a Lean project or paper and needs clarification.\n- The requested action would overwrite user work outside the target project tree.\n\n-- AGENTS --\n# Manuscript-Only Context Policy\n\n## Source authority\n\nFor manuscript-facing statements, the sole mathematical source is\n`sources/manuscript/main.tex`.\nThe local summary and locator record is `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`. Do not modify\nthe manuscript from this project.\n\nEvery claim described as coming from the manuscript must identify an exact\nline range in `main.tex`. Repository history, earlier chat context, and other\nartifacts are not substitute sources.\n\n## Context firewall\n\nDo not introduce, assume, or attribute to the manuscript any gLU,\nrefinement, layer-coherence, or finite-coordinate construction unless it is\npresent in the cited manuscript lines or is explicitly introduced as a new\nauxiliary assumption. A Lean file compiling does not make its unstated\nphysical or operator-algebraic hypotheses part of the manuscript.\n\nClassify each item as one of:\n\n1. **manuscript-derived**: exact source locator and no stronger conclusion;\n2. **auxiliary assumption**: explicitly labelled and used only conditionally;\n3. **Lean infrastructure**: reusable formal mathematics that makes no source\n   claim; or\n4. **legacy exploratory work**: retained for reference but unavailable as\n   support for a manuscript-derived assertion.\n\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md` records the current legacy items.\n\n## Current formalization boundary\n\nThe target discussed in the manuscript is the short-range regional-algebra\nstatement at `main.tex:1740-1752`; its proposed proof is at\n`main.tex:1891-2004`. Keep separate the asserted algebraic identification,\nthe product-state/GNS steps, the unformalized operator-algebraic completion and\nspatial tensor-product steps, and the trace conclusion. A tracial state is\nappropriate only for the finite pure-sector case. The infinite pure-sector\ntarget instead calls for a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight when the\nrequired hypotheses and von Neumann infrastructure are supplied.\n\n## Aristotle task packets\n\nEvery new task under `aristotle_tasks/` must begin with exactly one context\ndeclaration:\n\n- `Source basis: main.tex:<line range>` for a manuscript-facing task; or\n- `Infrastructure only:` for reusable Lean material that makes no manuscript\n  claim.\n\nIt must then list every auxiliary assumption, state its exact Lean target, and\nsay whether its conclusion is conditional. A task may not cite a legacy\nartifact as its source basis. Use `aristotle_tasks/TEMPLATE.md` for new\npackets.\n\n## Verification and reporting\n\nReport Lean compilation separately from mathematical validation. For each\nmanuscript-facing milestone, state which source clauses are represented, which\nassumptions remain external, and which analytic/von Neumann-algebraic claims\nare not yet formalized in the pinned Mathlib version.\n\n-- packet --\n# P38 — concrete quasilocal C-star model from the algebraic limit representation\n\n## Context declaration\n\n- **Source basis:** `main.tex:1915-1924`\n\n## Mathematical target\n\nCreate only `AristotleCandidates/ConcreteQuasilocalCStarModel.lean`. Combine\nP37's compatible algebraic-limit operator representation with the checked\nclosed operator-range construction. Given an explicitly faithful isometric\nrepresentation of the dyadic/algebraic local limit, construct its concrete\nclosed C-star range and prove the dense embedding and finite-stage formulas.\n\n## Boundary\n\nThe representation remains an explicit input unless constructed in the file.\nDo not claim an infinite tensor-product Hilbert space, a regional C-star\nisomorphism, GNS factorization, normality, or factor classification. No\n`sorry`, `admit`, or user axiom.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n`lake build AristotleCandidates.ConcreteQuasilocalCStarModel` succeeds and the\nresponse names the exact faithfulness/isometry assumptions.\n\n-- progress --\n  complex star-algebra equivalence; hence a nontracial product state cannot\n  become tracial after an algebraic coordinate identification.\n- `FMT-001` — the normalized trace on a nonempty finite full matrix algebra is\n  an algebraic tracial state.  Positivity is proved by the explicit\n  trace-of-a-star-square sum-of-squares identity.\n- `FSC-001` — a finite corner equipped with a star isomorphism to a full matrix\n  algebra inherits that algebraic tracial state.\n- `FTT-001` — the finite algebraic tensor product of two matrix factors is\n  identified by the Kronecker star isomorphism, and its normalized trace\n  factorizes on elementary tensors; tensoring with a matrix identity preserves\n  the trace.\n- `FAM-001` — if `R` has an algebraic tracial state, then its finite matrix\n  amplification `Matrix n n R` has the normalized diagonal tracial state.\n  Positivity is checked as a finite sum of coefficient-algebra star-squares.\n  An explicit `StarAlgEquiv` transports this finite type-I product trace to a\n  target algebra.\n- `TMT-001` — the finite type-I product is now identified canonically, not\n  supplied as an assumption: `Matrix n n ℂ ⊗[ℂ] R` is star-isomorphic to\n  `Matrix n n R`.  Its trace factors on elementary tensors as the normalized\n  matrix trace times the coefficient trace.\n- `DTT-001` — the actual dyadic finite-stage system is constructed as\n  `A₀ = ℂ`, `Aₙ₊₁ = Matrix (Fin 2) (Fin 2) Aₙ`, with diagonal identity-qubit\n  embeddings.  Its normalized traces are compatible with every stage map,\n  and their algebraic directed limit carries the induced algebraic tracial\n  state.\n- FPU-001 — for every nonempty finite matrix factor, its algebraic tensor\n  product with the concrete dyadic algebraic limit carries the checked product\n  tracial state.  The theorem includes the elementary-tensor formula\n  tr_p(a) times qubitUHFTrace(u).\n- FPU-002 — any supplied regional `StarAlgEquiv` into that finite-pure\n  product model induces an algebraic tracial state on the regional algebra,\n  with the checked formula on inverse images of elementary tensors.\n- `AIT-001` — compatible unital cyclic finite-stage functionals glue to a\n  unital cyclic linear functional on mathlib's algebraic directed direct\n  limit.\n- `AIT-002` — compatible stagewise star-algebra maps induce an involutive,\n  additive, multiplication-reversing star on that algebraic direct limit.\n  Compatible algebraic tracial states then glue to an ordinary algebraic\n  tracial state there.\n- `SPC-001` — an adapted Hamel basis identifies the label space with finitely\n  supported X/Z coordinate labels and maps the singleton coordinate labels to\n  the chosen basis vectors.\n- ASC-001 — for a selected set of Z coordinates, Lean now identifies the\n  corresponding coordinate subspace both as the span of those Z basis\n  vectors and as the finite words with no X coordinate and no Z coordinate\n  outside the selected set.  This is the exact linear selection criterion\n  required before comparing the regional stabilizer state with the\n  pure/mixed product state.\n- FQS-001 — Lean now reads the recursive finite-qubit Pauli label as its\n  finite X/Z coordinate list and proves that the pure/mixed product-state\n  indicator is exactly the selected-Z condition: a pure coordinate has no X\n  exponent, and a mixed coordinate is the identity label.  Under an arbitrary\n  supplied adapted coordinate basis, that condition is literal membership in\n  the selected-Z stabilizer submodule.  The coherent direct-limit state theorem\n  has a corresponding selected-Z indicator form on source basis words.\n- `PCC-001` — a phase-corrected additive map of Pauli labels respects word\n  multiplication and star exactly when it satisfies explicit multiplier and\n  involution equations.  This isolates the cocycle data that symplecticity\n  alone does not supply.  The cross-algebra word-basis extension is now also\n  checked: its two compatibility equations and a specified Pauli-word basis\n  yield a genuine `StarAlgEquiv`.\n- `SCC-001` — the quotient of two multiplier phases is an additive character,\n  calibrating by an additive character preserves the multiplier equation, and\n  every additive character on a vector subspace extends to the ambient vector\n  space along a complementary subspace.  This is the exact algebraic\n  stabilizer-sign calibration: the finite-field character is self-conjugate,\n  and calibration preserves both Pauli-word compatibility equations.  The\n  concrete finite-support quadratic phase is now independently formalized;\n  relating it to the actual regional stabilizer convention remains separate.\n- QPR-001 — the explicit ZMod 4 finite-support quadratic refinement has its\n  polarization and diagonal identities fully checked.  Its discrepancy pairing\n  is additive in both words, symmetric for a symmetric discrepancy, and has\n  additive order two.  These facts instantiate the cross-algebra Pauli-word\n  multiplication and involution certificates and, with a supplied word-basis\n  linear equivalence, a `StarAlgEquiv`.\n- QPT-001 — the concrete finite-support phase theorem now composes that\n  `StarAlgEquiv` with the finite-pure times qubit-UHF trace transport: whenever\n  the regional Pauli-word exponents, involution phases, and word-basis map are\n  supplied, Lean constructs both the star-algebra equivalence and its induced\n  algebraic tracial state with the elementary-tensor product formula.  The\n  Pauli-word interface has been weakened from `Ring` to `Semiring`, the exact\n  structure carried by the concrete algebraic UHF direct limit.\n- PTO-001 — two isometries whose range projections sum to the identity rule\n  out even an algebraic unital cyclic trace.  This is the checked obstruction\n  behind the infinite-pure-sector case; the tracial-state theorem is therefore\n  deliberately limited to a finite pure matrix factor.\n- ALT-001 — compatible finite-stage `StarAlgEquiv`s and compatible target\n  algebraic tracial states induce a source-side algebraic tracial state on the\n  corresponding quasi-local direct limit.  The theorem evaluates it exactly\n  on every finite-stage representative.  The concrete finite-pure UHF trace\n  now has the matching finite-stage elementary-tensor formula.\n- FQP-001 — every finite stage of the dyadic qubit tower now has an explicit\n  ordered Pauli-word `StarSystem` and a complex Pauli-word basis.  The\n  identity-qubit embeddings preserve its words, basis vectors, multiplication\n  multipliers, and star multipliers under coherent label maps.  Thus the UHF\n  target-side finite-stage word hypotheses are no longer abstract assumptions;\n  assembling them into one global target word system remains open.\n- PMS-001 — for every Boolean sequence specifying whether each newly adjoined\n  qubit is pure or maximally mixed, the corresponding finite product\n  functionals are algebraic states, are compatible with all dyadic embeddings,\n  and induce an algebraic state on the UHF direct limit.  Their values on every\n  finite Pauli word are proved recursively: pure coordinates permit only zero\n  X exponent, while mixed coordinates permit only the identity word.  The\n  all-mixed specialization is proved equal to the previously checked UHF\n  tracial state.  Lean also proves that even the one-qubit pure specialization\n  is not cyclic and hence not an algebraic tracial state.\n- PTR-001 — this nontraciality is now proved at arbitrary finite and\n  direct-limit coordinates: if any Boolean coordinate is pure, explicit\n  matrix units witness a failure of cyclicity.  Consequently the algebraic\n  UHF product state is tracial if and only if every coordinate is maximally\n  mixed, in which case it is the checked UHF trace.\n- CBL-001 — finite-stage star-algebra equivalences need not be assumed\n  coherent on every algebra element: if the source connecting maps, target\n  vectors, and coordinate maps agree on a specified source basis, Lean proves\n  the full commuting square by linear-basis extensionality.\n- CPS-001 — a phase-corrected finite coordinate system now has a concrete\n  direct-limit state interface.  Coherent source basis transport, phases, and\n  finite Pauli labels imply full-stage equivalence coherence and hence a\n  star-algebra equivalence to the algebraic qubit-UHF direct limit; the induced\n  source state evaluates a basis word as its phase times the pure/mixed Pauli\n  selection indicator.  The all-mixed specialization pulls the UHF trace back\n  to a source algebraic tracial state; more generally its pure/mixed pullback\n  is tracial exactly when every coordinate is mixed.  Constructing the\n  regional inputs remains open.\n- ACS-001 — if supplied finite-stage regional data identify selected target\n  Z-coordinates with the actual source stabilizer words, Lean derives the\n  exact phase-calibrated stabilizer expectation formula on every source basis word.\n  The stronger form states that hypothesis as literal membership in the\n  selected-Z submodule of an adapted coordinate basis.  Thus the remaining\n  geometric work is isolated to constructing and coherently transporting that\n  coordinate data from a separately supplied finite-coordinate realization,\n  rather than to a hidden infinite-algebra assumption. The manuscript does not\n  presently supply that realization; see `SOURCE-CONTRACT.md`.\n- ALS-001 — compatible finite-stage star-algebra equivalences transport\n  compatible algebraic states (not only tracial states) to a quasi-local\n  algebraic direct limit.  This is the exact state-level interface required by\n  a coherent regional stabilizer-coordinate construction.\n- WBE-001 — if Pauli words form a complex linear basis, checking a\n  complex-linear map on every pair of basis words proves multiplicativity on\n  all finite sums.  The corresponding conjugate-linear basis argument proves\n  star preservation; a bijective unital map satisfying both checks is packaged\n  as a star-algebra equivalence.\n- ALC-001 — a compatible family of finite-stage star-algebra maps induces a\n  star-algebra map from the algebraic directed limit.  Compatible finite-stage\n  star-algebra equivalences induce a star-algebra equivalence of the two\n  algebraic limits.  The C-star norm extension remains open.\n- CSE-001 — once a source and target are supplied as complex C-star algebras,\n  Lean proves that every complex `StarAlgEquiv` is isometric and therefore\n  continuous with continuous inverse.  A continuous target functional pulls\n  back continuously along it.  This does not construct C-star norms or\n  completions for the algebraic direct-limit models.\n- CCF-001 — the pinned Mathlib supports the finite dyadic stages as genuine\n  C-star algebras and supports closure/minimality once an ambient C-star\n  algebra is supplied.  It does not provide the C-star inductive-limit\n  construction for the algebraic dyadic limit; this is recorded as an\n  explicit missing API rather than hidden behind an axiom.\n- NMC-001 — supplied finite C-star realizations of the coherent coordinate\n  maps are isometric and continuous, and the commuting inclusion squares\n  preserve the norm estimates.  The resulting dense-subalgebra estimate is\n  exactly the analytic input needed before extending the algebraic map to a\n  completion; no completion is constructed here.\n- BMC-001 — for a star subalgebra of bounded operators on a Hilbert space,\n  Lean now constructs the algebraic bicommutant as a concrete\n  `VonNeumannAlgebra`, with monotonicity, minimality, membership, and\n  idempotence lemmas.  The analytic strong/weak double-commutant theorem is\n  still explicitly absent from the pinned Mathlib and remains separate.\n- CCC-001 — from an explicitly supplied isometric star representation of a\n  pre-C-star algebra into bounded operators, Lean constructs the closed\n  operator-range C-star algebra, its isometric dense embedding, and the\n  uniqueness equivalence between two such concrete completions.  The faithful\n  operator representation of the project-specific algebraic limit is still a\n  separate obligation.\n\n## The exact theorem still to prove\n\nThe Overleaf short-range theorem asserts a regional von Neumann algebra\nisomorphism to a type-I factor tensor a hyperfinite type-II1 factor.  It is\nnot yet formalized as a theorem because the supplied manuscript proof leaves\nthe following essential premises open:\n\n1. a finite-support adapted symplectic coordinate map whose finite-stage\n   source bases, phase correction, and target Pauli labels are coherent along\n   an exhaustion;\n2. identification of the actual regional Pauli multiplier and involution\n   exponents with the finite-support coordinate discrepancy, followed by\n   calibration to the actual stabilizer character;\n3. the induced quasi-local C-star isomorphism;\n4. factorization of the regional state into pure and normalized-tracial\n   coordinate states; and\n5. a normal/spatial extension to the respective bicommutants.\n\nThe current pinned mathlib version includes preliminary abstract and concrete\nvon Neumann-algebra definitions, but lacks a finished API for infinite von\nNeumann tensor products, normal states/traces, hyperfiniteness, and factor\nclassification.  The project therefore does not introduce any axiom for the\ntarget theorem.\n\n## Last verification\n\n```text\nlake build\nlake build Formalization.AlgebraicLimitStateTransport Formalization.QubitMatrixState Formalization.FinitePureMixedQubitState Formalization\nlake build Formalization.TraceTransport Formalization\nlake build Formalization.CoherentBasisEquiv Formalization.CoherentPhaseQubitState Formalization\nlake build Formalization.PureCoordinateNotTracial Formalization\nlake build Formalization.AdaptedStabilizerSubspace Formalization\nlake build Formalization.FiniteQubitPauliSelection Formalization.CoherentPhaseQubitState Formalization\nlake build Formalization.CStarEquivExtension Formalization\nlake build AristotleCandidates.CStarInductiveLimitFeasibility AristotleCandidates.FiniteCoordinateMapNormControl\nlake env lean /tmp/stabilizer_hyperfinite_axioms_run4.lean\npdflatex -interaction=nonstopmode -halt-on-error .../001-tensor-product-trace.tex\n```\n\nLean reports only `propext`, `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound` for the\nchecked tensor-amplification, concrete dyadic direct-limit trace,\nfinite-support quadratic-phase, induced-trace, finite-qubit Pauli-word,\npure/mixed product-state, basis-level coherence, phase-corrected direct-limit\nequivalence/state transport, and properly-infinite trace-obstruction theorems.\nNo source theorem contains `sorry`, `admit`, or a user-declared axiom.  The\nblueprint build output is kept under `/tmp`, not in this source project.\n\n-- reviews --\nreviews/P37-algebraic-limit-operator-representation-closeout.md\n\n-- response dirs --\naristotle_responses\naristotle_responses/P37-extracted\naristotle_responses/P37-extracted/aristotle-stabilizer_hyperfinite-y537ze25_aristotle\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '-- Aristotle reference --' && sed -n '1,320p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/references/aristotle-cli.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '-- P37 closeout --' && sed -n '1,360p' reviews/P37-algebraic-limit-operator-representation-closeout.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '-- repository maps --' && find . -maxdepth 2 -type f \\\\( -name 'lakefile.*' -o -name 'lean-toolchain' -o -name '*Axiom*' -o -name 'README.md' \\\\) -printf '%p\\\\n' | sort && printf '%s\\\\n' '-- task status tail --' && tail -180 history/logs/20260806T184917Z-09be9e9d-tasks.log\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"printf '%s\\\\n' '-- Aristotle reference --' && sed -n '1,320p' <original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/references/aristotle-cli.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '-- P37 closeout --' && sed -n '1,360p' reviews/P37-algebraic-limit-operator-representation-closeout.md && printf '%s\\\\n' '-- repository maps --' && find . -maxdepth 2 -type f \\\\( -name 'lakefile.*' -o -name 'lean-toolchain' -o -name '*Axiom*' -o -name 'README.md' \\\\) -printf '%p\\\\n' | sort && printf '%s\\\\n' '-- task status tail --' && tail -180 history/logs/20260806T184917Z-09be9e9d-tasks.log\"","aggregated_output":"-- Aristotle reference --\n# Aristotle CLI reference\n\n## Setup\n\n- Install: `uv tool install aristotlelib`\n- Verify: `aristotle --version`\n- Authenticate: set `ARISTOTLE_API_KEY` in the shell environment, or use the skill wrapper `scripts/aristotle-auth.sh`.\n\nIf the key is stored near the end of `~/.bashrc`, noninteractive commands may not see it because many `.bashrc` files contain:\n\n```bash\ncase $- in\n    *i*) ;;\n      *) return;;\nesac\n```\n\nIn that case, do not rely on `source ~/.bashrc`. Use the wrapper, which reads a literal `export ARISTOTLE_API_KEY=...` assignment without printing it:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize input.md\n```\n\n## Core commands\n\nSubmit a Lean project for sorry filling or proof search:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh submit \"Fill in all sorries\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\nFormalize a TeX or paper source:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh formalize paper.tex --wait --destination output.tar.gz\n```\n\nList recent jobs:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh list --limit 10\n```\n\nDownload a completed job:\n\n```bash\n<original-home>/.codex/skills/aristotle-cli/scripts/aristotle-auth.sh download <project-id> --destination result.tar.gz\n```\n\nPass the key directly for one command only:\n\n```bash\naristotle submit \"My prompt\" --api-key \"$ARISTOTLE_API_KEY\" --project-dir ./my-lean-project --wait\n```\n\n## Recommended project loop\n\n1. Inspect the Lean project locally.\n2. Run the narrowest local build or test that exercises the target files.\n3. Submit a focused Aristotle prompt against the project directory.\n4. Wait for the run if the expected result is a concrete patch.\n5. Download or inspect the produced artifact.\n6. Rebuild locally and repeat with a tighter prompt if needed.\n\n## Prompt shape\n\nKeep prompts short and file-bound. Good prompts mention:\n\n- the exact project directory\n- the theorem or file to work on\n- whether to fill `sorry`s, formalize a statement, or debug a failing proof\n- what counts as a useful partial result\n\nAvoid vague prompts such as \"improve this project\" unless the scope is intentionally broad.\n-- P37 closeout --\n# P37 closeout review — algebraic-limit operator representation\n\n## Decision\n\n**Status: proved at the packet's stated conditional infrastructure scope.**\n\nThe reviewed module is `AristotleCandidates/AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation.lean`.\nIt is byte-identical to the candidate in the quarantined Aristotle archive at\n`aristotle_responses/P37-extracted/aristotle-stabilizer_hyperfinite-y537ze25_aristotle/AristotleCandidates/AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation.lean`.\nThe archive was downloaded to `aristotle_responses/` and was never built,\nupdated, or used for dependency fetching.\n\nP37 is explicitly **Infrastructure only**, so it makes no manuscript claim.\n\n## Exact declarations and premises\n\nFor a preorder `ι` with `IsDirectedOrder ι` and `Nonempty ι`, a family of\ncomplex semirings/star-rings/algebras `A`, a directed system of connecting\n`StarAlgHom`s `f`, and a supplied complete complex inner-product space `H`,\nthe module declares:\n\n- `StabilizerHyperfinite.algebraicLimitOperatorRep`:\n  `DirectLimit A f →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (H →L[ℂ] H)`, from supplied\n  `rho : ∀ i, A i →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (H →L[ℂ] H)` and the explicit compatibility premise\n  `compat : ∀ i j (h : i ≤ j) x, rho j (f i j h x) = rho i x`.\n- `algebraicLimitOperatorRep_of` and `algebraicLimitOperatorRep_of_apply`:\n  the exact finite-stage operator and vector formulas.\n- `algebraicLimitOperatorRep_stage_congr` and\n  `algebraicLimitOperatorRep_congr_of_eq`: representative independence.\n- `algebraicLimitOperatorRep_unique`: uniqueness among star-algebra maps with\n  the same finite-stage values.\n- `connecting_injective_of_stage_rep_injective`: stagewise injectivity of\n  `rho` implies injectivity of every connecting map, using `compat`.\n- `algebraicLimitOperatorRep_injective`: the induced limit representation is\n  injective under the additional explicit premise\n  `h_inj : ∀ i, Function.Injective (rho i)`.\n- `QubitOperatorRepresentationData`, whose fields are the supplied dyadic\n  stage representations and their compatibility with `qubitTensorPowerMap`,\n  together with `limitRep`, `limitRep_of`, `limitRep_of_apply`,\n  `rho_congr_of_eq`, `limitRep_unique`, and the conditional\n  `limitRep_injective`.\n\nThe route uses the existing `algebraicLimitStarAlgHom` construction. No new\nmathematical premise was silently added: faithfulness is expressly conditional\non stagewise injectivity; separate injectivity of the connecting maps is\nderived rather than assumed.\n\n## Scope classification\n\n| Classification | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| proved | The compatible algebraic direct-limit representation, finite-stage formulas, representative independence, uniqueness, and conditional faithfulness described above. |\n| partial | None at P37's stated scope. |\n| blocked | No blocking Lean/API issue for this algebraic construction. |\n| failed | None. |\n\nThe packet's mathematical target is met only in this conditional algebraic\nform. It does **not** construct an infinite tensor-product Hilbert space,\nC-star norm/completion, operator-norm closure, GNS representation, bicommutant,\nvon Neumann closure, trace/weight, gLU, or refinement data.\n\n## Adversarial review\n\nNo defect was found in the reviewed scope. In particular, the potentially\nstrong faithfulness claim is justified by Mathlib's `DirectLimit.induction₂`,\nwhich puts two limit representatives into a common stage before applying the\nexplicit `h_inj` hypothesis. Representative-independence uses `compat` in the\ncorrect orientation. The module does not introduce `sorry`, `admit`, a user\naxiom, `opaque`, `unsafe`, `implemented_by`, or `native_decide`.\n\nResidual risk: Lean compilation and the axiom audit validate this algebraic\ninterface, not the unformalized analytic/operator-algebraic continuation.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `lake build AristotleCandidates.AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation` — passed.\n- `lake build AristotleCandidates.AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation Formalization` — passed.\n- `lake build` — passed.\n- `lake env lean /tmp/stabilizer_hyperfinite_p37_axioms.lean` — passed;\n  all eight audited public declarations depend only on `propext`,\n  `Classical.choice`, and `Quot.sound`.\n- `rg -n '\\b(sorry|admit|axiom|opaque|unsafe|implemented_by|native_decide)\\b' AristotleCandidates/AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation.lean` — no matches.\n- `git diff --check` — passed.\n\n## Cleanup and worktree state\n\nKept intentionally:\n\n- `AristotleCandidates/AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation.lean`, the sole\n  reviewed integration;\n- `aristotle_responses/P37-algebraic-limit-operator-representation.tar.gz` and\n  its extracted quarantine snapshot, preserving the raw remote response;\n- this closeout review.\n\nAll pre-existing modified and untracked files were preserved. No manuscript\nfile was modified, and no commit or push was made.\n\n-- repository maps --\n./README.md\n./aristotle_tasks/README.md\n./lakefile.lean\n./le