{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_13","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1915,1924p' sources/manuscript/main.tex && printf '\\\\n-- source contract pertinent --\\\\n' && sed -n '1,240p' SOURCE-CONTRACT.md && printf '\\\\n-- candidate source declaration --\\\\n' && sed -n '1,72p' AristotleCandidates/ConcreteQuasilocalCStarModel.lean\"","aggregated_output":"    We can now define an $\\ast$-isomorphism $\\a: \\cP_I \\rightarrow \\cA_\\G$ on the\n    infinite Pauli algebra $\\cP_I$ \\LS{Define} via\n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\a(X_i) = P(x_i),\\quad \\a(Z_i) = P(z_i)\n    \\end{equation}\n    and extending it to a homomorphism \\LS{Expand construction}.\n    This in turn defines a state on $\\cP_I$\n    \\begin{equation}\n        \\tilde{\\w} = \\w_\\cS \\circ \\a.\n    \\end{equation}\n\n-- source contract pertinent --\n# Manuscript source contract\n\n## Authoritative source\n\n- Manuscript: `sources/manuscript/main.tex`\n- Title in source: *Infinite Magic and the Continuum* (`main.tex:117`)\n- Formalization policy: `AGENTS.md`\n\nThis project does not treat its own Lean files, prior agent prompts, or the\nsurrounding repository as mathematical source authority.\n\n## Source clauses in scope\n\n| Source locator | What the manuscript says | Formalization status |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `main.tex:1671-1679` | Defines short-range order through a local symplectic basis, with `W_Gamma = span{z_i : i in P}` and finite support. | Requires a precise Lean model of the infinite Pauli/symplectic data. |\n| `main.tex:1740-1752` | States the short-range regional-algebra theorem, with an asserted factorization `A_Gamma` into a pure-sector type-I factor and a core factor that is finite type I or hyperfinite `II_1`. | Target statement; not established by current Lean artifacts. |\n| `main.tex:1915-1920` | Proposes an adapted symplectic basis and a star-isomorphism from an infinite Pauli algebra to `A_Gamma`. | External assumption/gap until the source construction is formalized. |\n| `main.tex:1927-1931` | Writes a pure/mixed product density state. | Finite density-matrix fragments are formalized; the infinite model is not. |\n| `main.tex:1945-1967` | Proposes the GNS/tensor-factor and type conclusions. | Conditional GNS transport is formalized; analytic product and type conclusions are not. |\n| `main.tex:1970-2004` | Proposes quasi-equivalence and normal-folium transport. | Not formalized. |\n\nThe wording above is a locator-oriented paraphrase, not a replacement for the\nmanuscript.\n\n## Permitted auxiliary assumptions\n\nAny manuscript-facing Lean theorem that needs one of the following must expose\nit in the theorem statement or task packet:\n\n1. a completed C-star or von Neumann algebra model for the infinite Pauli\n   system and its regional algebra;\n2. the asserted star-isomorphism from that model to the regional algebra;\n3. the product-state formula and hypotheses making the GNS representation a\n   spatial tensor product;\n4. the von Neumann closure, normality, and quasi-equivalence assumptions needed\n   to transport the claimed factor type; and\n5. any hyperfiniteness or trace theorem used for the core factor.\n\nNone of these assumptions is supplied merely by the phrase “short-range\norder” in the current manuscript source.\n\n## Trace correction\n\nThe manuscript-facing target must distinguish two cases:\n\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is finite-dimensional, a normalized tracial\n  state can be obtained from the finite matrix trace together with the core\n  trace;\n- if the pure-sector Hilbert space is infinite-dimensional, the appropriate\n  conclusion is a faithful normal semifinite trace/weight on the `II_infinity`\n  target, not a tracial state.\n\nThis is a correction to the intended theorem contract, not a claim that the\ncurrent manuscript already states or proves the corrected dichotomy.\n\n## Legacy firewall\n\nEarlier gLU/refinement/layer-coherence material is listed in\n`CONTEXT-LEDGER.md`. It is retained, but it is neither a source clause nor an\nimplicit assumption of this formalization.\n\n-- candidate source declaration --\nimport AristotleCandidates.AlgebraicLimitOperatorRepresentation\nimport AristotleCandidates.ConcreteCStarCompletion\n\n/-!\n# A concrete quasilocal C⋆-model from the algebraic limit representation\n\n**Context declaration.** Source basis: `manuscript/main.tex:1915-1924` (the\nquasilocal algebra obtained from the algebraic local limit together with a\nrepresentation of it).  Everything below is *conditional* on explicitly\nsupplied data; no infinite tensor-product Hilbert space is constructed here.\n\nThis module combines\n\n* the compatible algebraic-limit operator representation\n  `StabilizerHyperfinite.algebraicLimitOperatorRep` (a star-algebra\n  homomorphism `DirectLimit A f →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (H →L[ℂ] H)` induced by a compatible\n  family of finite-stage representations `rho i : A i →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (H →L[ℂ] H)`),\n  with\n* the checked *closed operator-range* construction of\n  `Formalization.ConcreteCStarCompletion` (the topological closure of the range\n  of a star-algebra homomorphism inside the bounded operators of a Hilbert\n  space, which is a genuine C⋆-algebra).\n\nThe result is a **concrete** quasilocal C⋆-algebra\n\n`quasilocalCStarAlgebra rho compat = ((algebraicLimitOperatorRep rho compat).range).topologicalClosure`,\n\na closed star-subalgebra of `H →L[ℂ] H`, together with a canonical star-algebra\nhomomorphism from the algebraic limit into it which\n\n* has **dense range** (`denseRange_quasilocalEmbedding`);\n* satisfies the **finite-stage formula**\n  `quasilocalEmbedding rho compat (of i x) = rho i x`\n  (`coe_quasilocalEmbedding_of`);\n* is **injective (faithful)** as soon as every stage representation is\n  injective (`injective_quasilocalEmbedding`);\n* realizes the concrete algebra as the closure of the union of the finite-stage\n  operator ranges (`coe_quasilocalCStarAlgebra_eq_closure_iUnion`) — the precise\n  quasilocality statement;\n* is **minimal**: the concrete algebra is the least closed star-subalgebra of\n  `H →L[ℂ] H` containing all finite-stage operator ranges\n  (`quasilocalCStarAlgebra_isLeast`).\n\n## Exact hypotheses\n\nTwo packages of assumptions are used, both explicit:\n\n* `rho`, `compat`: a supplied Hilbert space `H`, supplied stage representations\n  `rho i : A i →⋆ₐ[ℂ] (H →L[ℂ] H)` and the compatibility\n  `rho j (f i j h x) = rho i x` with the connecting maps.  Faithfulness of the\n  limit representation is then *conditional* on `∀ i, Function.Injective (rho i)`.\n* `IsometricStageRepresentation`: the same data for *normed* stage algebras,\n  with the additional field `norm_rho : ∀ i a, ‖rho i a‖ = ‖a‖`.  This is the\n  \"explicitly faithful isometric representation\" input: isometry of each stage\n  representation implies injectivity of each stage representation, hence\n  faithfulness of the induced limit representation, and it yields the\n  finite-stage *norm* formula `‖embedding (of i x)‖ = ‖x‖`.\n\n## What is *not* claimed\n\nNo infinite tensor-product Hilbert space, no regional C⋆-isomorphism, no GNS\nfactorization, no normality, no bicommutant or factor classification, and no\nstatement that the concrete algebra is a UHF algebra.\n-/\n\nnamespace StabilizerHyperfinite\nnamespace ConcreteQuasilocalCStarModel\n\nopen Formalization.ConcreteCStarCompletion\n\n/-! ## The concrete quasilocal C⋆-algebra of a compatible stage representation -/\n\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
